freshing outline and it's very  user-friendly we wanted it to be uh to  be that above all  so with that i like to turn to the last  thing that we really try to focus on and  and that's the freedom to teach  uh we think that one of the things that  students have  or that teachers have have  had deprived of them  is is the freedom to to teach to uh to  not be weighed down by a lot of other  tasks that they have to do and instead  to recover that love of learning that is  usually the reason why teachers got into  the profession in the first place um and  so  this this has that in mind it wants to  restore the freedom of teachers to teach  and what that means is that as dr  o'toole laid out  this doesn't provide dictates to  teachers it provides guidance certain  parameters and some things that we think  could be good for a teacher to think  about in teaching about american history  and civics that by no means closes off  other options or or uh or unnecessarily  narrows or cramps the style of the the  teacher that the teacher wants to employ  in in their instruction so  uh with that i'd like to  walk us through how we might plan with  the 1776 curriculum um looking at it  okay an example and uh for that like to  go to a middle school uh unit  um  and uh this is it says two of our  initial uh lessons or units and and  they're more forthcoming then eventually  in the early part of 2022 we'll have  enough to canvas all of american history  so um this is how we've laid it out  we've tried to simplify it streamline it  make it easy to read in the first place  so  in addition to a little overview at the  beginning outlining the lessons that are  entailed and pacing guidance for how  many classes a lesson might take  we provide some thoughts on why we're  teaching this unit some main enduring  ideas that we might bring away take away  from it and then some ideas about what  the teacher should consider in the  process  um and this provides kind of a high  level overview of okay