ferent  images that we can pull and the internet  is a great resource here we weigh out  here's some images that will go with  this lesson a good quick search engine  engine search we'll bring these up quite  readily then we also have stories that  we have for the american heart uh little  anecdotes and tropes that that that  again students can carry with them and  they can punctuate the lesson and then  you know if we enter into a a a  class and we want to see a lot of  questioning um  you know a lot of not just those  questions of students about the who what  and when and where also the why  questions and the how questions and how  do you know that what does that show us  about this period of history or about  human beings and in human society so um  these are all in there and we think that  you try and you know weave those all  together you don't have to use all of it  it has to be  fine-tuned to your classroom you want  teachers to have autonomy to do that  one of the areas that that we try to uh  provide a little bit more guidance on  are some of those sticky points in  history or civic instruction are there  some things that we oftentimes get wrong  or sometimes there can be a little  thorny to deal with and so we've offered  some thoughts on how to navigate those  and we put them in  the order in which we think that lesson  would naturally proceed and these are  called the keys to the lesson if we get  these things right and carefully think  about them that you're going to end up  with a with a pretty good uh lesson on  the other end and one that's accurate  and true to history um so what does this  look like in the classroom uh we have  this section on pedagogy and taking a  guess through the classroom and what it  looks like and and and what it means is  that the teacher is leading the teacher  is is bringing the students alongside  alongside her and telling a story that's  that's the main thing that we want to do  with this curriculum is tell a story  from